The BUDT Solver Engine: A Complete Theoretical Framework for Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory


ouadi Maakoul 

AI : Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

We present a complete theoretical treatment of the Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT) solver engine, a constrained partially observable Markov decision process framework in which belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, calibrated action selection, and constrained policy optimisation are placed on equal footing. We establish contraction properties of the bounded-resource Bellman operator under compact resource spaces, prove existence and measurable selection of optimal policies via Berge's Maximum Theorem under an interior-feasibility condition, derive distribution-free coverage guarantees via adaptive conformal inference with decaying step sizes, establish Bayesian and frequentist regret bounds that are both sublinear in the horizon, and provide a two-time-scale stochastic approximation analysis for the joint actor–critic–dual–encoder system under explicit boundedness assumptions. The framework integrates learned belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, constrained policy optimisation, and conformal calibration into a single coherent Lagrangian architecture with provable safety and near-optimality guarantees.

---

1. Problem Formulation

1.1 The Augmented State Space

Let 𝒮 be a Polish state space, 𝒪 a Polish observation space, and 𝒜 a compact metric action space. The agent operates in a resource-constrained partially observable environment. The augmented state is

X_t = (m_t, \rho_t) \in \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R},

where m_t \in \mathcal{M} is the memory state (a sufficient statistic of the observation history) and \rho_t \in \mathcal{R} \subset \mathbb{R}^K_+ is the resource vector. We assume:

· \mathcal{M} is a compact metric space.
· \mathcal{R} = [0, \rho_{\max}]^K is compact and convex.
· \mathcal{A} is a compact metric space.

The environment is characterised by a transition kernel P(ds' \mid s, a), an observation kernel O(do \mid s, a), and a reward function r : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}. The resource cost is \mathcal{C} : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}^K_+.

The belief state b_t \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) evolves via the Bayes update

\mathcal{B}(b, a, o')(ds) = \frac{O(do' \mid s, a) \, b(ds)}{\int_{\mathcal{S}} O(do' \mid s, a) \, b(ds')}.

The memory state is derived from the belief via the compressor m_t = \phi_{\mathrm{comp}}(b_t, \rho_t). We assume that the memory is a sufficient statistic for the belief in the following sense.

Assumption 1 (Memory Sufficiency). For all beliefs b, b' \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) with \phi_{\mathrm{comp}}(b, \rho) = \phi_{\mathrm{comp}}(b', \rho), the value function satisfies V^\star(b, \rho) = V^\star(b', \rho) for all \rho \in \mathcal{R}.

1.2 The Constrained Objective

The agent selects actions a_t \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t, \rho_t), where

\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m, \rho) = \{a \in \mathcal{A} : \mathcal{C}(a, m) \le \rho\}.

The resource-adjusted return is

J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \big(r(s_t, a_t) - \lambda^\top \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t)\big)\right],

where \gamma \in (0, 1) and \lambda \in \mathbb{R}^K_+ is the vector of Lagrange multipliers. The resource constraint is

\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t)\right] \le \rho_0.

1.3 The Bounded-Resource Bellman Equation

The optimal value function V^\star : \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} satisfies

V^\star(m, \rho) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m, \rho)} \mathbb{E}\left[r(s, a) - \lambda^\top \mathcal{C}(a, m) + \gamma V^\star(m', \rho') \mid b, a\right],

where the expectation is taken with respect to the belief b associated with memory m and the observation kernel. We approximate V^\star by V_\theta : \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathbb{R} with parameters \theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p, and the policy by \pi_\phi : \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}) with parameters \phi \in \Phi \subseteq \mathbb{R}^q.

