2. Mathematical Preliminaries and Assumptions

2.1 Assumptions

Assumption 2 (Spaces). \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{O} are Polish, \mathcal{A} is compact metric, \mathcal{M} is compact metric, \mathcal{R} = [0, \rho_{\max}]^K is compact convex.

Assumption 3 (Continuity). The kernels P(ds' \mid s, a), O(do \mid s, a), the reward r(s, a), and the cost \mathcal{C}(a, m) are continuous in all arguments. The belief update \mathcal{B}(b, a, o') is continuous in the weak topology on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}).

Assumption 4 (Interior Feasibility). For every (m, \rho) \in \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{R}, there exists a \in \mathcal{A} and a uniform \delta > 0 such that \mathcal{C}(a, m) \le \rho - \delta \mathbf{1} componentwise.

Assumption 5 (Approximation Error). There exists \varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}} < \infty such that

\sup_{V \in \mathcal{V}} \|\Pi_\Theta V - V\|_{L^2(\mu)} \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}.

Assumption 6 (Boundedness). r, \mathcal{C}, and V_\theta are uniformly bounded on their domains.

Assumption 7 (Lipschitz Continuity). r, \mathcal{C}, V_\theta, \pi_\phi, \phi_{\mathrm{enc}}, \phi_{\mathrm{comp}} are Lipschitz continuous with constants L_r, L_c, L_V, L_\pi, L_{\mathrm{enc}}, L_{\mathrm{comp}}.

Assumption 8 (Learning Rates). The learning rates satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions

\sum_t \alpha_t = \infty, \quad \sum_t \alpha_t^2 < \infty, \quad \sum_t \beta_t = \infty, \quad \sum_t \beta_t^2 < \infty.

Assumption 9 (Ergodicity). For every stationary policy \pi, the Markov chain \{X_t\} is ergodic with stationary distribution \mu_\pi.

2.2 Continuity of the Belief Update

Lemma 1 (Continuity of the Bayes Map). Under Assumption 3, the map (b, a, o') \mapsto \mathcal{B}(b, a, o') is continuous in the weak topology on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}).

Proof. Let (b_n, a_n, o_n') \to (b, a, o') weakly. For any bounded continuous f : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R},

\int f(s) \, \mathcal{B}(b_n, a_n, o_n')(ds) = \frac{\int f(s) O(do_n' \mid s, a_n) \, b_n(ds)}{\int O(do_n' \mid s, a_n) \, b_n(ds')}.

By Assumption 3, O(do' \mid s, a) is continuous in s and a, so s \mapsto O(do_n' \mid s, a_n) converges uniformly to s \mapsto O(do' \mid s, a). Since b_n \to b weakly and the integrands are bounded continuous, the numerator and denominator converge to their respective limits. The denominator is bounded below by \eta_{\min} > 0, so the quotient converges. \square

Corollary 1 (Total Variation Bound). For any b, b' \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}),

\|\mathcal{B}(b, a, o') - \mathcal{B}(b', a, o')\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \frac{2\|O\|_\infty}{\eta_{\min}} \|b - b'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

2.3 Continuity of the Feasible Correspondence

Lemma 2 (Berge's Maximum Theorem Application). Under Assumptions 3 and 4, the correspondence \Gamma(m, \rho) = \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m, \rho) is continuous (both upper and lower hemicontinuous).

Proof. Upper hemicontinuity. The graph

\mathrm{Gr}(\Gamma) = \{(m, \rho, a) : \mathcal{C}(a, m) \le \rho\}

is closed because \mathcal{C} is continuous. Since \mathcal{A} is compact, closedness of the graph implies upper hemicontinuity.

Lower hemicontinuity. Let (m_n, \rho_n) \to (m, \rho) and a \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m, \rho). By Assumption 4, there exists a^\delta \in \mathcal{A} and \delta > 0 such that \mathcal{C}(a^\delta, m) \le \rho - \delta \mathbf{1}. Define a_n = (1 - t_n) a + t_n a^\delta with t_n \to 0^+ chosen so that \mathcal{C}(a_n, m_n) \le \rho_n. Such t_n exists because \mathcal{C} is continuous and \rho_n \to \rho. Then a_n \to a and a_n \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_n, \rho_n), establishing lower hemicontinuity. \square

