5. Conformal Calibration

5.1 Non-Conformity Scores and Feasible Set

Given a prior policy \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}, define the non-conformity score

S_t(a) = 1 - \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}(a \mid m_t).

Let \mathcal{W} be a calibration window of non-conformity scores \{S_j\}_{j \in \mathcal{W}}. The empirical quantile at level 1 - \alpha_t is

\hat{q}_t = \mathrm{Quantile}\left(\{S_j\}_{j \in \mathcal{W}}, 1 - \alpha_t\right).

The conformal feasible set is

\mathcal{C}_t = \{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t, \rho_t) : S_t(a) \le \hat{q}_t\}.

5.2 Fallback Handling

If \mathcal{C}_t = \emptyset, we include the least non-conforming valid action:

\mathcal{C}_t = \left\{\arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t, \rho_t)} S_t(a)\right\}.

This preserves the coverage guarantee by ensuring \mathcal{C}_t \ne \emptyset while minimising the deviation from the conformal threshold.

5.3 Adaptive Conformal Inference with Decaying Step Size

The ACI update is

\alpha_{t+1} = \alpha_t + \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}} - \mathrm{err}_t), \qquad
\mathrm{err}_t = \mathbf{1}\{a^\star(m_t) \notin \mathcal{C}_t\},

with clipping \alpha_{t+1} \leftarrow \mathrm{clip}(\alpha_{t+1}, 0, 1) and decaying step size

\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} = \frac{\gamma_0}{\sqrt{t}}, \qquad \gamma_0 > 0.

Remark 3. The decaying step size is essential for the regret analysis: a constant step size yields linear cumulative miscoverage in the worst case, which would destroy the sublinearity of the Bayesian regret bound. The choice \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} \propto 1/\sqrt{t} yields an average miscoverage of order O(1/\sqrt{T}).

5.4 Coverage Guarantee

Theorem 3 (ACI Coverage). Under the decaying step size \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} = \gamma_0/\sqrt{t}, the ACI update ensures

\left|\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathrm{err}_t - \alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}\right| \le O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right) + \frac{n_{\mathrm{fallback}}}{T},

where n_{\mathrm{fallback}} is the number of fallback steps.

Proof. The ACI recursion can be rewritten as

\alpha_{t+1} = \alpha_t + \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}} - \mathrm{err}_t).

Telescoping and using 0 \le \alpha_{t+1} \le 1,

\sum_{t=1}^T \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}} - \mathrm{err}_t) = \alpha_{T+1} - \alpha_1.

Taking absolute values and using \sum_{t=1}^T \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} = \gamma_0 \sum_{t=1}^T t^{-1/2} = \Theta(\sqrt{T}),

\left|\sum_{t=1}^T \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}} - \mathrm{err}_t)\right| \le \max\{\alpha_1, 1 - \alpha_1\}.

Dividing by \sum_{t=1}^T \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} = \Theta(\sqrt{T}) yields the O(1/\sqrt{T}) rate. Fallback steps contribute an additive correction n_{\mathrm{fallback}}/T. \square

Corollary 3 (Sublinear Cumulative Miscoverage). Under the decaying step size, the cumulative miscoverage satisfies

\left|\sum_{t=1}^T \mathrm{err}_t - T \alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}\right| \le O(\sqrt{T}) + n_{\mathrm{fallback}}.

This is the key ingredient for the sublinear Bayesian regret bound.

