6. Bayesian Regret

6.1 Information Ratio

Definition 1. The information ratio is

\Gamma_t = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t]^2}{\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t]},

where \Delta_t is the instantaneous regret and \mathcal{I}_t is the information gain.

Lemma 4 (Bounded Information Ratio Under Conformal Restriction). For the conformal-restricted Thompson sampling policy with decaying ACI step size,

\Gamma_t^{\mathcal{C}} \le 2K\sigma^2 + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{t}}\right).

Proof. The information ratio for the unrestricted Thompson sampling policy satisfies \Gamma_t \le 2K\sigma^2 (Russo–Van Roy, 2016). Restricting to \mathcal{C}_t can decrease both the numerator (instantaneous regret) and the denominator (information gain). To bound the effect, decompose the regret into in-set and out-of-set contributions:

\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t] = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t \mathbf{1}\{a_t \in \mathcal{C}_t\}]}_{\text{in-set}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t \mathbf{1}\{a_t \notin \mathcal{C}_t\}]}_{\text{out-of-set}}.

The in-set regret is bounded by the unrestricted information-ratio bound because conditioning on a_t \in \mathcal{C}_t can only reduce the effective action set. The out-of-set regret is bounded by the ACI miscoverage rate, which decays as O(1/\sqrt{t}) under the decaying step size (Corollary 3). Combining,

\Gamma_t^{\mathcal{C}} \le \frac{(\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t^{\mathrm{in}}] + O(t^{-1/2}))^2}{\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t^{\mathrm{in}}]} \le 2K\sigma^2 + O(t^{-1/2}),

where the last inequality uses the standard inequality (x + y)^2 \le x^2 + 2xy + y^2 and the boundedness of \mathbb{E}[\Delta_t^{\mathrm{in}}] and \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t^{\mathrm{in}}]. \square

6.2 Regret Bound

Theorem 4 (Bayesian Regret). Under Assumptions 2–7 and the decaying ACI step size,

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{2K\sigma^2 \, H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) \, T} + O(\sqrt{T}),

where H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) is the entropy of the prior over the optimal arm.

Proof. Decompose

R_T = \underbrace{\sum_{t : a_t \in \mathcal{C}_t} \Delta_t}_{R_T^{\mathrm{in}}} + \underbrace{\sum_{t : a_t \notin \mathcal{C}_t} \Delta_t}_{R_T^{\mathrm{out}}}.

For the in-set rounds, the standard information-ratio bound (Lemma 4) gives

\mathbb{E}[R_T^{\mathrm{in}}] \le \sqrt{2K\sigma^2 \, H(A^\star) \, T} + O(\sqrt{T}).

For the out-of-set rounds, the ACI rate (Corollary 3) ensures

\mathbb{E}[R_T^{\mathrm{out}}] \le O(\sqrt{T}) + n_{\mathrm{fallback}} \cdot \Delta_{\max}.

Since n_{\mathrm{fallback}} \le T and the fallback steps contribute at most O(\sqrt{T}) under the decaying step size, the total is

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{2K\sigma^2 \, H(A^\star) \, T} + O(\sqrt{T}). \qquad \square

Corollary 4 (Prior Utility). The LLM prior improves the bound relative to uniform if and only if

H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) < \log K,

which is checkable by Monte-Carlo sampling from the prior before any data arrive.

Remark 4 (Misspecification). If the prior is misspecified, the same formal bound holds with H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) replaced by the entropy of the prior (not of the true optimal arm). The bound is therefore robust to misspecification, at the cost of a potentially larger constant. The O(\sqrt{T}) term now absorbs the ACI overhead, restoring sublinearity.

