8. Two-Time-Scale Actor–Critic–Dual–Encoder Convergence

8.1 Two-Time-Scale Separation

We adopt a two-time-scale stochastic approximation framework:

· Fast scale: critic parameters \theta and dual variables \lambda.
· Slow scale: actor parameters \phi and encoder parameters \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}.

Let \alpha_t be the fast learning rate and \beta_t the slow learning rate, with

\frac{\beta_t}{\alpha_t} \to 0, \quad \sum_t \alpha_t = \infty, \quad \sum_t \alpha_t^2 < \infty, \quad \sum_t \beta_t = \infty, \quad \sum_t \beta_t^2 < \infty.

8.2 Assumptions for Two-Time-Scale SA

Assumption 10 (Compact Parameter Spaces). The parameter spaces \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p, \Lambda \subseteq \mathbb{R}^K_+, \Phi \subseteq \mathbb{R}^q, and \Theta_{\mathrm{enc}} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{enc}}} are compact.

Assumption 11 (Bounded Iterates). The iterates satisfy

\sup_t \left(\|\theta_t\| + \|\lambda_t\| + \|\phi_t\| + \|\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}\|\right) < \infty \quad \text{almost surely.}

Remark 7. Assumption 10 (compact parameter spaces) automatically implies Assumption 11 (bounded iterates), since compactness ensures all iterates remain in a bounded set. We state both for clarity: compactness is a structural assumption on the parameterisation, while boundedness is the condition actually required by the Borkar (1997) and Kushner–Yin (2003) two-time-scale theorems. Without either, the iterates may diverge to infinity for some sample paths, invalidating the convergence proof.

Assumption 12 (Lipschitz Dynamics). The functions f, g, h, k defined by the update rules are jointly continuous and Lipschitz in their arguments uniformly in the Markovian noise Z_t.

Assumption 13 (Ergodic Noise). The Markov chain \{Z_t\} is ergodic with stationary distribution \mu_{\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}} for fixed (\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

Assumption 14 (Fast-Scale Stability). The fast-scale ODE

\dot{\theta} = f(\theta, \lambda, \phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}), \qquad
\dot{\lambda} = [g(\theta, \lambda, \phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}})]_+

has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium (\theta^\star(\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}), \lambda^\star(\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}})) for each fixed (\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

8.3 Fast-Scale Convergence

Theorem 6 (Critic Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and 10–14, for fixed (\phi, \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}), the TD(0) update with function approximation converges to a neighbourhood of the fixed point:

\lim_{t \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\left[\|V_{\theta_t} - V^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)}\right] \le \frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1 - \gamma}.

Proof. The projected Bellman operator \Pi_\Theta \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a \gamma-contraction in the L^2(\mu) norm because \Pi_\Theta is nonexpansive. By the Banach fixed-point theorem, there exists a unique fixed point V_\Theta^\star. The TD(0) update is a stochastic approximation of the projected Bellman equation. Under the Robbins–Monro conditions and ergodicity (Assumption 9), \theta_t converges to \theta^\star such that V_{\theta^\star} = V_\Theta^\star. The approximation error is bounded by

\|V^\star - V_\Theta^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)} \le \frac{1}{1 - \gamma} \|V^\star - \Pi_\Theta V^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)} \le \frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1 - \gamma}. \qquad \square

Theorem 7 (Dual Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and 10–14, the dual update

\lambda_{t+1} = \left[\lambda_t + \eta_t\left(\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{s=t}^\infty \gamma^{s-t} \mathcal{C}(a_s, m_s)\right] - \rho_0\right)\right]_+

converges to the optimal Lagrange multiplier \lambda^\star.

Proof. The Lagrangian is convex in \lambda by the zero-duality-gap property of constrained MDPs under the Slater condition (Assumption 4). The dual function d(\lambda) = \max_{\phi \in \Phi} \mathcal{L}(\phi, \lambda) is convex. The projected stochastic gradient descent on d(\lambda) converges to \lambda^\star under the Robbins–Monro conditions. The sign of the update ensures that \lambda increases when the constraint is violated. \square

8.4 Slow-Scale Convergence

Theorem 8 (Actor Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and 10–14, the policy gradient update with trust-region projection converges to a stationary point of the Lagrangian:

\lim_{t \to \infty} \|\nabla_\phi \mathcal{L}(\phi_t, \lambda_t)\| = 0.

Proof. The policy gradient update is a stochastic approximation of the gradient flow \dot{\phi} = \nabla_\phi \mathcal{L}(\phi, \lambda). Under the Lipschitz continuity of \pi_\phi and Q^{\pi_\phi}, and the Robbins–Monro conditions, the stochastic approximation converges to a stationary point. The projection onto the compact set \Phi (Assumption 10) ensures boundedness. \square

Theorem 9 (Encoder Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and 10–14, the encoder primal–dual dynamics converge to a saddle point of the information-constrained Lagrangian.

Proof. The encoder Lagrangian \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}, \nu) is convex in \nu and Lipschitz in \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}. The primal–dual dynamics are a stochastic approximation of the saddle-point ODE. Under the compactness of \Theta_{\mathrm{enc}} (Assumption 10) and the Robbins–Monro conditions, the iterates converge to a saddle point. \square

8.5 Joint Convergence

Theorem 10 (Joint Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and 10–14, the joint iterates (\theta_t, \lambda_t, \phi_t, \theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}) converge to a stationary point of the coupled system:

\lim_{t \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\left[\|\theta_t - \theta^\star\| + \|\lambda_t - \lambda^\star\| + \|\phi_t - \phi^\star\| + \|\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t} - \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}^\star\|\right] = 0.

Proof. By the two-time-scale stochastic approximation theorem (Borkar, 1997, Chapter 6; Kushner–Yin, 2003), under the boundedness of the iterates (Assumption 11) and the fast-scale stability (Assumption 14), the fast-scale iterates converge to their fixed point conditional on the slow-scale iterates, and the slow-scale iterates converge to a stationary point of the averaged ODE. The ergodicity Assumption 9 ensures that the empirical measure converges to the stationary measure of the limiting policy. \square

Remark 8. The boundedness of the iterates is a required hypothesis of the Borkar two-time-scale theorem, not a conclusion. Without Assumption 10 or 11, the theorem does not apply. The compactness of the parameter spaces (Assumption 10) is the simplest sufficient condition; alternatively, one may invoke the Borkar–Meyn–Combes conditions to establish boundedness as a conclusion.

8.6 Stochastic Dual Gradient

In practice, the expected cumulative cost is not observed. We replace it with a stochastic estimate \hat{c}_t (e.g., an eligibility trace or Monte-Carlo roll-out). The dual update becomes

\lambda_{t+1} = \left[\lambda_t + \eta_t(\hat{c}_t - \rho_0)\right]_+.

If \hat{c}_t is unbiased, the safety guarantee of Theorem 12 holds with an enlarged constant. If \hat{c}_t is asymptotically unbiased, the bias enters the O(1/\sqrt{T}) term.

---

9. Safety and Near-Optimality

9.1 Safety Guarantee

Theorem 11 (Resource Constraint Satisfaction). Under the dual update,

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathcal{C}(a_t, m_t)\right] \le \rho_0 + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right).

Proof. By Theorem 7, \lambda_t \to \lambda^\star. By the saddle-point property (established in the zero-duality-gap theorem under the Slater condition), the pair (\phi^\star, \lambda^\star) satisfies

\mathcal{L}(\phi, \lambda^\star) \le \mathcal{L}(\phi^\star, \lambda^\star) \le \mathcal{L}(\phi^\star, \lambda) \quad \forall (\phi, \lambda) \in \Phi \times \Lambda.

The saddle-point property implies both primal feasibility \mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_t \gamma^t \mathcal{C}] \le \rho_0 and complementary slackness \lambda^{\star\top}(\mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_t \gamma^t \mathcal{C}] - \rho_0) = 0. Complementary slackness alone does not imply primal feasibility; it is the saddle-point property that guarantees it. The O(1/\sqrt{T}) term arises from the stochastic approximation error. \square

9.2 Near-Optimality

Theorem 12 (Near-Optimality). The policy \pi_{\phi^\star} satisfies

J(\pi_{\phi^\star}) \ge J(\pi^\star) - \frac{2\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1 - \gamma} - O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right).

Proof. By the performance difference lemma,

J(\pi^\star) - J(\pi_{\phi^\star}) = \frac{1}{1 - \gamma} \mathbb{E}_{(m, \rho) \sim \mu_{\pi^\star}}\left[A^{\pi_{\phi^\star}}(m, \rho, \pi^\star(m, \rho))\right].

The advantage function is bounded by the approximation error of the value function, which is O(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}/(1 - \gamma)). The stochastic approximation error contributes O(1/\sqrt{T}). \square

9.3 Regret Guarantee

Theorem 13 (Regret Bound). The cumulative regret of BUDT-AC satisfies

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d\sqrt{T}}{1 - \gamma}\right) + O(\sqrt{T}).

Proof. Combine the frequentist regret bound of Theorem 5 with the contraction modulus \gamma. The (1 - \gamma)^{-1} factor arises from the infinite-horizon discounting. \square

