12. Concluding Remarks

The BUDT solver engine provides a complete algorithmic realisation of Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory, with rigorous convergence guarantees, safety constraints, and near-optimality bounds. The framework integrates learned belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, constrained policy optimisation, and conformal calibration into a single coherent Lagrangian architecture. The key theoretical contributions are:

1. Compact-resource contraction. The Bellman operator is a \gamma-contraction in the supremum norm under the compactness of \mathcal{R} (Assumption 2). The Perov/weighted-norm apparatus for unbounded state spaces is unnecessary and has been removed. The strict-depletion condition is retained only for the transient bound (Lemma 3) and is not required for the contraction or existence results.
2. Rigorous continuity. The interior-feasibility condition (Assumption 4) guarantees lower hemicontinuity of the feasible correspondence, enabling the application of Berge's Maximum Theorem and the Measurable Maximum Theorem for the existence of a measurable optimal policy.
3. Sublinear Bayesian regret. The ACI step size is scheduled to decay as \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t} = \gamma_0/\sqrt{t}, which yields O(\sqrt{T}) cumulative miscoverage (Corollary 3) and restores the sublinear Bayesian regret bound \sqrt{2K\sigma^2 H(A^\star) T} + O(\sqrt{T}). The information-ratio restriction to \mathcal{C}_t is justified explicitly (Lemma 4).
4. Optimal frequentist regret. Linear UCB inside the conformal set achieves the minimax-optimal rate \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}) plus the conformal overhead O(\sqrt{T}).
5. Bounded two-time-scale convergence. The two-time-scale stochastic approximation analysis includes the required boundedness assumption (Assumption 11) and compactness of parameter spaces (Assumption 10), closing the gap in the Borkar/Kushner–Yin theorem application.
6. Correct belief-based expectation. The Bellman operator integrates against the belief b(ds), not an undefined P(ds \mid m), with the memory sufficiency assumption (Assumption 1) providing the link between memory and belief.
7. Saddle-point safety guarantee. The safety proof cites the saddle-point property (not complementary slackness alone) to establish primal feasibility of the limit.

The framework is robust to misspecification of the LLM prior, handles the conformal fallback without breaking coverage, and treats the encoder as a constrained optimisation problem within the same Lagrangian architecture. The conceptual contribution—placing resource constraints, belief compression, and calibrated selection on equal footing with classical POMDP control—is thus given a rigorous mathematical foundation with provable guarantees.


Appendices: Detailed Proofs and Mathematical Corrections

Appendix A: Continuity of the Belief Update and Feasible Correspondence

A.1 Belief Update Continuity

Let \mathcal{B}:\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S})\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{O}\to\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) denote the Bayes belief update:

\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')(ds)=\frac{O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds)}{\int_{\mathcal{S}}O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds')}.

Lemma A.1 (Continuity of the Bayes Map). Under Assumption 3, the map (b,a,o')\mapsto\mathcal{B}(b,a,o') is continuous in the weak topology on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}).

Proof. Let (b_n,a_n,o_n')\to(b,a,o') weakly. For any bounded continuous f:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R},

\int f(s)\,\mathcal{B}(b_n,a_n,o_n')(ds)=\frac{\int f(s)O(do_n'\mid s,a_n)\,b_n(ds)}{\int O(do_n'\mid s,a_n)\,b_n(ds')}.

By Assumption 3, O(do'\mid s,a) is continuous in s and a, so s\mapsto O(do_n'\mid s,a_n) converges uniformly to s\mapsto O(do'\mid s,a). Since b_n\to b weakly and the integrands are bounded continuous, the numerator and denominator converge to their respective limits. The denominator is bounded below by \eta_{\min}>0, so the quotient converges. \square

Corollary A.1 (Total Variation Bound). For any b,b'\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}),

\|\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')-\mathcal{B}(b',a,o')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le\frac{2\|O\|_\infty}{\eta_{\min}}\|b-b'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

A.2 Feasible Correspondence Continuity

Lemma A.2 (Berge's Maximum Theorem Application). Under Assumptions 3 and 4, the correspondence \Gamma(m,\rho)=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho) is continuous (both upper and lower hemicontinuous).

Proof. Upper hemicontinuity. The graph of \Gamma is

\mathrm{Gr}(\Gamma)=\{(m,\rho,a):\mathcal{C}(a,m)\le\rho\}.

This set is closed because \mathcal{C} is continuous and \le is a closed relation. Since \mathcal{A} is compact, closedness of the graph implies upper hemicontinuity.

Lower hemicontinuity. Let (m_n,\rho_n)\to(m,\rho) and a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho). By Assumption 4, there exists a^\delta\in\mathcal{A} and \delta>0 such that \mathcal{C}(a^\delta,m)\le\rho-\delta\mathbf{1}. Define a_n=(1-t_n)a+t_n a^\delta with t_n\to0^+ chosen so that \mathcal{C}(a_n,m_n)\le\rho_n. Such t_n exists because \mathcal{C} is continuous and \rho_n\to\rho. Then a_n\to a and a_n\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_n,\rho_n), establishing lower hemicontinuity. \square

Remark A.1. The interior-feasibility condition (Assumption 4) is essential for lower hemicontinuity. Without it, the feasible correspondence may fail to be lower hemicontinuous, and Berge's Maximum Theorem does not apply. This is the standard Slater-type constraint qualification in constrained optimisation, widely used in constrained reinforcement learning analyses to obtain strong duality and to bound the optimal dual variable.

A.3 Measurable Selection

Lemma A.3 (Measurable Maximum Theorem). Under Assumptions 3 and 4, the argmax correspondence

\Gamma^\star(m,\rho)=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)}\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V(m',\rho')\mid b,a\right]

is nonempty, compact-valued, and upper hemicontinuous. Consequently, there exists a measurable selector \pi^\star.

Proof. By Lemma A.2, \Gamma is continuous with compact values. The integrand is continuous in a by Assumption 3 and bounded by Assumption 6. By Berge's Maximum Theorem, \Gamma^\star is nonempty, compact-valued, and upper hemicontinuous. The Measurable Maximum Theorem (Aliprantis–Border, Theorem 18.19) then guarantees the existence of a measurable selector. \square

