Appendix B: The Perov Extension — When It Is Needed and When It Is Not

B.1 The Compact-Resource Case: Perov Is Unnecessary

Proposition B.1 (Sufficiency of the Supremum Norm). Under Assumption 2 (\mathcal{R}=[0,\rho_{\max}]^K compact) and Assumption 6 (boundedness), the Bellman operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a \gamma-contraction in the standard supremum norm \|\cdot\|_\infty. The Perov/weighted-norm apparatus is not required.

Proof. The contraction proof (Theorem 1) uses only the \gamma-discount and the boundedness of the value function. The compactness of \mathcal{R} ensures that \|\cdot\|_\infty is finite and that trajectories remain bounded by construction via the projection \Pi_{\mathcal{R}}. No weighted norm is needed. \square

Remark B.1. The Perov contraction theorem is designed specifically for unbounded state spaces and unbounded reward functions. When the reward function is unbounded, combining an appropriate weighted supremum norm with the Perov contraction theorem yields a unique fixed point of the Bellman operator under weaker conditions than existing approaches. The uniform growth-rate condition that Perov relaxes is only relevant when the state space is unbounded. Under the compactness assumption of the BUDT framework, this machinery is solving a problem that cannot occur.

B.2 The Unbounded-Resource Case: Perov Is Necessary

If the compactness assumption is relaxed and \mathcal{R}=\mathbb{R}^K_+ is unbounded, the Perov extension becomes necessary. We include it here for completeness.

Definition B.1 (Vector-Valued Metric). A vector-valued metric on a set X is a map d:X\times X\to\mathbb{R}^K_+ satisfying:

1. d(x,y)=0 iff x=y,
2. d(x,y)=d(y,x),
3. d(x,z)\le d(x,y)+d(y,z) (componentwise).

Theorem B.1 (Perov Fixed Point Theorem). Let (X,d) be a complete generalized metric space and T:X\to X satisfy

d(Tx,Ty)\le Md(x,y)

for some nonnegative matrix M\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}_+ with spectral radius \rho(M)<1. Then T has a unique fixed point x^\star\in X, and for any x_0\in X, the sequence x_{n+1}=T(x_n) converges to x^\star.

Theorem B.2 (Perov Contraction of \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} in the Unbounded Case). Under Assumptions 3 and 6′, where Assumption 6′ allows unbounded rewards, and the weight condition

\gamma\sup_{m,\rho,a}\frac{\mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')\mid m,\rho,a]}{\kappa(\rho)}<1,

with \kappa(\rho)=\exp(\sum_{i=1}^K\kappa_i\rho_i) and \kappa_i>0, the operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a Perov contraction in the weighted norm \|\cdot\|_\kappa. There exists a nonnegative matrix M with spectral radius \rho(M)<1 such that

\|\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_1-\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_2\|_\kappa\le M\|V_1-V_2\|_\kappa.

Proof. For any V_1,V_2\in\mathcal{V}_\kappa and any (m,\rho,a),

\begin{aligned}
&\left|\mathbb{E}[r-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}+\gamma V_1(m',\rho')]-\mathbb{E}[r-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}+\gamma V_2(m',\rho')]\right|\\
&\le\gamma\mathbb{E}\left[|V_1(m',\rho')-V_2(m',\rho')|\mid m,\rho,a\right]\\
&\le\gamma\|V_1-V_2\|_\kappa\,\mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')\mid m,\rho,a]\\
&=\gamma\|V_1-V_2\|_\kappa\,\frac{\mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')\mid m,\rho,a]}{\kappa(\rho)}\,\kappa(\rho).
\end{aligned}

Taking the supremum over (m,\rho,a) and dividing by \kappa(\rho),

|(\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_1)(m,\rho)-(\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_2)(m,\rho)|\le B\,\|V_1-V_2\|_\kappa\,\kappa(\rho),

where B=\gamma\sup_{m,\rho,a}\mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')]/\kappa(\rho). Under the weight condition, B<1, so M=B\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top has spectral radius B<1. The Perov fixed point theorem applies. \square

Remark B.2. The weight condition is satisfied when the resource dynamics are mean-reverting or when the growth rate of the value function is sub-exponential. For the compact case \mathcal{R}=[0,\rho_{\max}]^K, we may take \kappa_i\to0, recovering the standard supremum norm.

B.3 Transient Bound Under Strict Depletion

Lemma B.3 (Expected Hitting Time). Under Assumption 3 (strict depletion: c_{\min}>\varepsilon_{\max}), let \tau_\delta=\inf\{t\ge0:\|\rho_t\|_1\le\delta\}. Then

\mathbb{E}[\tau_\delta]\le\frac{\|\rho_0\|_1}{c_{\min}-\varepsilon_{\max}}+O(1).

Proof. The resource update is \rho_{t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{R}}(\rho_t-\mathcal{C}(a_t,m_t)+\epsilon_t). The drift satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\|\rho_{t+1}\|_1-\|\rho_t\|_1\mid\mathcal{F}_t]\le-\|\mathcal{C}(a_t,m_t)\|_1+\|\epsilon_t\|_1\le-(c_{\min}-\varepsilon_{\max})<0.

The result follows from the optional stopping theorem applied to the stopped supermartingale \|\rho_{t\wedge\tau_\delta}\|_1+(c_{\min}-\varepsilon_{\max})(t\wedge\tau_\delta). \square

