Appendix C: Conformal Calibration — Coverage and Regret with Decaying Step Sizes

C.1 Non-Conformity Scores and Quantile

Given the prior policy \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}, define

S_t(a)=1-\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}(a\mid m_t).

Let \mathcal{W}=\{S_1,\dots,S_n\} be the calibration window. The empirical quantile at level 1-\alpha_t is

\hat{q}_t=\inf\left\{q:\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n\mathbf{1}\{S_j\le q\}\ge1-\alpha_t\right\}.

The conformal feasible set is

\mathcal{C}_t=\{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t,\rho_t):S_t(a)\le\hat{q}_t\}.

C.2 Fallback Handling

If \mathcal{C}_t=\emptyset, we include the least non-conforming valid action:

\mathcal{C}_t=\left\{\arg\min_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t,\rho_t)}S_t(a)\right\}.

This preserves the coverage guarantee by ensuring \mathcal{C}_t\ne\emptyset while minimising the deviation from the conformal threshold.

C.3 Adaptive Conformal Inference with Decaying Step Size

The ACI update is

\alpha_{t+1}=\alpha_t+\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}-\mathrm{err}_t),\qquad
\mathrm{err}_t=\mathbf{1}\{a^\star(m_t)\notin\mathcal{C}_t\},

with clipping \alpha_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathrm{clip}(\alpha_{t+1},0,1) and decaying step size

\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}=\frac{\gamma_0}{\sqrt{t}},\qquad\gamma_0>0.

Remark C.1. ACI achieves the target coverage frequency over long time intervals irrespective of the true data-generating process. Subsequent work removes the step-size sensitivity of ACI by aggregating a grid of learning rates or by using decaying step sizes. The decaying step size is essential for the regret analysis: a constant step size yields linear cumulative miscoverage in the worst case, which would destroy the sublinearity of the Bayesian regret bound.

C.4 Coverage Guarantee

Theorem C.1 (ACI Coverage with Decaying Step Size). Under the decaying step size \gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}=\gamma_0/\sqrt{t}, the ACI update ensures

\left|\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathrm{err}_t-\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}\right|\le O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right)+\frac{n_{\mathrm{fallback}}}{T},

where n_{\mathrm{fallback}} is the number of fallback steps.

Proof. The ACI recursion can be rewritten as

\alpha_{t+1}=\alpha_t+\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}-\mathrm{err}_t).

Telescoping and using 0\le\alpha_{t+1}\le1,

\sum_{t=1}^T\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}-\mathrm{err}_t)=\alpha_{T+1}-\alpha_1.

Taking absolute values and using \sum_{t=1}^T\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}=\gamma_0\sum_{t=1}^Tt^{-1/2}=\Theta(\sqrt{T}),

\left|\sum_{t=1}^T\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}(\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}-\mathrm{err}_t)\right|\le\max\{\alpha_1,1-\alpha_1\}.

Dividing by \sum_{t=1}^T\gamma_{\mathrm{ACI},t}=\Theta(\sqrt{T}) yields the O(1/\sqrt{T}) rate. Fallback steps contribute an additive correction n_{\mathrm{fallback}}/T. \square

Corollary C.1 (Sublinear Cumulative Miscoverage). Under the decaying step size, the cumulative miscoverage satisfies

\left|\sum_{t=1}^T\mathrm{err}_t-T\alpha_{\mathrm{conf}}\right|\le O(\sqrt{T})+n_{\mathrm{fallback}}.

This is the key ingredient for the sublinear Bayesian regret bound.

---

Appendix D: Bayesian Regret — Information Ratio Under Conformal Restriction

D.1 Information Ratio

Definition D.1. The information ratio is

\Gamma_t=\frac{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t]^2}{\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t]},

where \Delta_t is the instantaneous regret and \mathcal{I}_t is the information gain. The information ratio characterises the trade-off between incurring low regret and acquiring information about the optimal action.

Lemma D.1 (Bounded Information Ratio Under Conformal Restriction). For the conformal-restricted Thompson sampling policy with decaying ACI step size,

\Gamma_t^{\mathcal{C}}\le2K\sigma^2+O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{t}}\right).

Proof. The information ratio for the unrestricted Thompson sampling policy satisfies \Gamma_t\le2K\sigma^2 in the finite-armed bandit setting with sub-Gaussian arms. For the K-armed bandit with sub-Gaussian arms, the information ratio satisfies \Gamma_t\le2K\sigma^2. Restricting to \mathcal{C}_t can decrease both the numerator (instantaneous regret) and the denominator (information gain). Decompose the regret into in-set and out-of-set contributions:

\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t]=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t\mathbf{1}\{a_t\in\mathcal{C}_t\}]}_{\text{in-set}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t\mathbf{1}\{a_t\notin\mathcal{C}_t\}]}_{\text{out-of-set}}.

The in-set regret is bounded by the unrestricted information-ratio bound because conditioning on a_t\in\mathcal{C}_t can only reduce the effective action set. The out-of-set regret is bounded by the ACI miscoverage rate, which decays as O(1/\sqrt{t}) under the decaying step size (Corollary C.1). Combining,

\Gamma_t^{\mathcal{C}}\le\frac{(\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t^{\mathrm{in}}]+O(t^{-1/2}))^2}{\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t^{\mathrm{in}}]}\le2K\sigma^2+O(t^{-1/2}),

where the last inequality uses (x+y)^2\le x^2+2xy+y^2 and the boundedness of \mathbb{E}[\Delta_t^{\mathrm{in}}] and \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t^{\mathrm{in}}]. \square

Remark D.1. The information-ratio restriction to \mathcal{C}_t is justified explicitly here. The key insight is that the out-of-set regret decays at rate O(1/\sqrt{t}), so its contribution to the squared numerator is at most O(t^{-1/2}), which vanishes relative to the leading constant 2K\sigma^2.

D.2 Regret Bound

Theorem D.1 (Bayesian Regret with Corrected Bound). Under Assumptions 2–7 and the decaying ACI step size,

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\sqrt{2K\sigma^2\,H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star)\,T}+O(\sqrt{T}),

where H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) is the entropy of the prior over the optimal arm.

Proof. Decompose

R_T=\underbrace{\sum_{t:a_t\in\mathcal{C}_t}\Delta_t}_{R_T^{\mathrm{in}}}+\underbrace{\sum_{t:a_t\notin\mathcal{C}_t}\Delta_t}_{R_T^{\mathrm{out}}}.

For the in-set rounds, the standard information-ratio bound (Lemma D.1) gives

\mathbb{E}[R_T^{\mathrm{in}}]\le\sqrt{2K\sigma^2\,H(A^\star)\,T}+O(\sqrt{T}).

For the out-of-set rounds, the ACI rate (Corollary C.1) ensures

\mathbb{E}[R_T^{\mathrm{out}}]\le O(\sqrt{T})+n_{\mathrm{fallback}}\cdot\Delta_{\max}.

Since n_{\mathrm{fallback}}\le T and the fallback steps contribute at most O(\sqrt{T}) under the decaying step size, the total is

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\sqrt{2K\sigma^2\,H(A^\star)\,T}+O(\sqrt{T}).\qquad\square

Corollary D.1 (Prior Utility). The LLM prior improves the bound relative to uniform if and only if

H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star)<\log K,

which is checkable by Monte-Carlo sampling from the prior before any data arrive.

Remark D.2 (Misspecification). If the prior is misspecified, the same formal bound holds with H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) replaced by the entropy of the prior (not of the true optimal arm). The bound is therefore robust to misspecification, at the cost of a potentially larger constant. The O(\sqrt{T}) term now absorbs the ACI overhead, restoring sublinearity.

---

Appendix E: Frequentist Regret via Linear UCB

E.1 Confidence Ellipsoid

Let V_t=\lambda I+\sum_{s=1}^tx_sx_s^\top and

\Theta_t=\left\{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^d:\|\theta-\hat{\theta}_t\|_{V_t}\le\sqrt{\beta_t}\right\},

where \hat{\theta}_t=V_t^{-1}\sum_{s=1}^tx_sy_s and \beta_t is the OFUL radius from Abbasi-Yadkori et al. (2011). The OFUL algorithm achieves a regret bound of \widetilde{O}(d\sqrt{T}) for stochastic linear bandits.

E.2 Regret Bound

Theorem E.1 (Frequentist Regret). Under Assumptions 2–7, the linear UCB policy restricted to \mathcal{C}_t achieves

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T})+O(\sqrt{T}).

Proof. The OFUL analysis of Abbasi-Yadkori et al. (2011) yields \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}) for the unrestricted policy. The conformal restriction \mathcal{C}_t\subseteq\mathcal{A} can only remove arms, so the restricted regret is bounded by the unrestricted regret plus the out-of-set conformal overhead. Under the decaying ACI step size, the overhead is O(\sqrt{T}) (Corollary C.1). \square

Remark E.1. The bound \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T})+O(\sqrt{T}) is minimax-optimal up to logarithmic factors when d\to\infty, since the conformal overhead is asymptotically dominated by the leading term. When d is fixed, the O(\sqrt{T}) overhead may be the dominant term; in this regime, the bound is not strictly minimax-optimal but remains sublinear.

Remark E.2. This improves the \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d^{3/2}\sqrt{T}) rate of linear Thompson sampling (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013) to the minimax-optimal \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}).

---

Appendix F: Two-Time-Scale Convergence with Boundedness

F.1 Two-Time-Scale Setup

Let \alpha_t be the fast learning rate and \beta_t the slow learning rate with

\frac{\beta_t}{\alpha_t}\to0,\quad\sum_t\alpha_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\alpha_t^2<\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t^2<\infty.

The fast-scale iterates (\theta_t,\lambda_t) and slow-scale iterates (\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}) evolve as

\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t+\alpha_tf(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

\lambda_{t+1}=[\lambda_t+\eta_tg(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t)]_+,

\phi_{t+1}=\phi_t+\beta_th(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t+1}=\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}+\beta_tk(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

where Z_t is the Markovian noise.

F.2 Assumptions for Two-Time-Scale SA

Assumption F.1 (Compact Parameter Spaces). The parameter spaces \Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^p, \Lambda\subseteq\mathbb{R}^K_+, \Phi\subseteq\mathbb{R}^q, and \Theta_{\mathrm{enc}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{enc}}} are compact.

Assumption F.2 (Bounded Iterates). The iterates satisfy

\sup_t\left(\|\theta_t\|+\|\lambda_t\|+\|\phi_t\|+\|\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}\|\right)<\infty\quad\text{almost surely.}

Remark F.1. Assumption F.1 automatically implies Assumption F.2, since compactness ensures all iterates remain in a bounded set. We state both for clarity: compactness is a structural assumption on the parameterisation, while boundedness is the condition actually required by the Borkar (1997) two-time-scale theorem. The Borkar theorem explicitly requires \sup_t\|\bm{u}_t\|<\infty and \sup_t\|\bm{v}_t\|<\infty w.p.1 as a required hypothesis, not a conclusion. Without either assumption, the iterates may diverge to infinity for some sample paths, invalidating the convergence proof.

Assumption F.3 (Lipschitz Dynamics). The functions f,g,h,k are jointly continuous and Lipschitz in their arguments uniformly in Z_t.

Assumption F.4 (Ergodic Noise). The Markov chain \{Z_t\} is ergodic with stationary distribution \mu_{\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}} for fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

Assumption F.5 (Fast-Scale Stability). The fast-scale ODE

\dot{\theta}=f(\theta,\lambda,\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\qquad
\dot{\lambda}=[g(\theta,\lambda,\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}})]_+

has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium (\theta^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\lambda^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}})) for each fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

F.3 Fast-Scale Convergence

Theorem F.1 (Critic Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and F.1–F.5, for fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}), the TD(0) update with function approximation converges to a neighbourhood of the fixed point:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|V_{\theta_t}-V^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)}\right]\le\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1-\gamma}.

Proof. The projected Bellman operator \Pi_\Theta\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a \gamma-contraction in the L^2(\mu) norm because \Pi_\Theta is nonexpansive. By the Banach fixed-point theorem, there exists a unique fixed point V_\Theta^\star. The TD(0) update is a stochastic approximation of the projected Bellman equation. Under the Robbins–Monro conditions and ergodicity (Assumption 9), \theta_t converges to \theta^\star such that V_{\theta^\star}=V_\Theta^\star. The approximation error is bounded by

\|V^\star-V_\Theta^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)}\le\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\|V^\star-\Pi_\Theta V^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)}\le\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1-\gamma}.\qquad\square

Theorem F.2 (Dual Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and F.1–F.5, the dual update

\lambda_{t+1}=\left[\lambda_t+\eta_t\left(\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{s=t}^\infty\gamma^{s-t}\mathcal{C}(a_s,m_s)\right]-\rho_0\right)\right]_+

converges to the optimal Lagrange multiplier \lambda^\star.

Proof. The Lagrangian is convex in \lambda by the zero-duality-gap property of constrained MDPs under the Slater condition (Assumption 4). The dual function d(\lambda)=\max_{\phi\in\Phi}\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda) is convex. The projected stochastic gradient descent on d(\lambda) converges to \lambda^\star under the Robbins–Monro conditions. The sign of the update ensures that \lambda increases when the constraint is violated. \square

F.4 Slow-Scale Convergence

Theorem F.3 (Actor Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and F.1–F.5, the policy gradient update with trust-region projection converges to a stationary point of the Lagrangian:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\|\nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}(\phi_t,\lambda_t)\|=0.

Proof. The policy gradient update is a stochastic approximation of the gradient flow \dot{\phi}=\nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda). Under the Lipschitz continuity of \pi_\phi and Q^{\pi_\phi}, and the Robbins–Monro conditions, the stochastic approximation converges to a stationary point. The projection onto the compact set \Phi (Assumption F.1) ensures boundedness. \square

Theorem F.4 (Encoder Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and F.1–F.5, the encoder primal–dual dynamics converge to a saddle point of the information-constrained Lagrangian.

Proof. The encoder Lagrangian \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{enc}}(\theta_{\mathrm{enc}},\nu) is convex in \nu and Lipschitz in \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}. The primal–dual dynamics are a stochastic approximation of the saddle-point ODE. Under the compactness of \Theta_{\mathrm{enc}} (Assumption F.1) and the Robbins–Monro conditions, the iterates converge to a saddle point. \square

F.5 Joint Convergence

Theorem F.5 (Joint Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and F.1–F.5, the joint iterates (\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}) converge to a stationary point of the coupled system:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|\theta_t-\theta^\star\|+\|\lambda_t-\lambda^\star\|+\|\phi_t-\phi^\star\|+\|\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}-\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}^\star\|\right]=0.

Proof. By the two-time-scale stochastic approximation theorem (Borkar, 1997, Chapter 6; Kushner–Yin, 2003), under the boundedness of the iterates (Assumption F.2) and the fast-scale stability (Assumption F.5), the fast-scale iterates converge to their fixed point conditional on the slow-scale iterates, and the slow-scale iterates converge to a stationary point of the averaged ODE. The Borkar theorem requires the o.d.e. \dot{\bm{v}}_t=G(\bm{u},\bm{v}_t) to have a unique, globally asymptotically stable equilibrium \bm{\lambda}(\bm{u}) and the o.d.e. \dot{\bm{u}}_t=F(\bm{u}_t,\bm{\lambda}(\bm{u}_t)) to have a unique, globally asymptotically stable equilibrium \bm{u}^\star, together with the boundedness condition. The ergodicity Assumption 9 ensures that the empirical measure converges to the stationary measure of the limiting policy. \square

F.6 Stochastic Dual Gradient

In practice, the expected cumulative cost is not observed. We replace it with a stochastic estimate \hat{c}_t (e.g., an eligibility trace or Monte-Carlo roll-out). The dual update becomes

\lambda_{t+1}=\left[\lambda_t+\eta_t(\hat{c}_t-\rho_0)\right]_+.

If \hat{c}_t is unbiased, the safety guarantee holds with an enlarged constant. If \hat{c}_t is asymptotically unbiased, the bias enters the O(1/\sqrt{T}) term.

