Appendix G: Safety and Near-Optimality — Saddle-Point Property

G.1 Zero Duality Gap and Saddle-Point Existence

Theorem G.1 (Zero Duality Gap Under Slater Condition). Under Assumptions 3 and 4 (Slater condition), the constrained MDP has zero duality gap:

\min_{\lambda\ge0}\max_{\phi\in\Phi}\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda)=\max_{\phi\in\Phi}\min_{\lambda\ge0}\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda).

Proof. The occupancy-measure representation of the constrained MDP is convex for stochastic policies. Under the Slater condition (Assumption 4), strong duality holds, and the saddle point exists:

\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda^\star)\le\mathcal{L}(\phi^\star,\lambda^\star)\le\mathcal{L}(\phi^\star,\lambda)\quad\forall(\phi,\lambda)\in\Phi\times\Lambda.

The Slater-type condition is standard in constrained RL analyses, where it is commonly used to obtain strong duality and to bound the optimal dual variable. Under the Slater condition, a useful property of the dual variable is that the sublevel sets are bounded. The dual variable is bounded in \Lambda=[0,\lambda_{\max}] with \lambda_{\max}=\mathbf{1}/((1-\gamma)\xi), where \xi is the feasibility margin. \square

Lemma G.1 (Bounded Optimal Dual Variable). Consider the constrained optimisation problem with thresholds \bm{\tau}=(\tau_1,\dots,\tau_I). Suppose the problem satisfies the Slater condition with margin \phi>0. Then the optimal dual variable \lambda^\star satisfies

\|\lambda^\star\|_1\le\frac{1}{\phi}.

Proof. This is Lemma 3 of the constrained RL analysis, which demonstrates that under the Slater-type condition, an optimal dual variable \lambda^\star is bounded by the reciprocal of the feasibility margin. \square

G.2 Safety Guarantee

Theorem G.2 (Resource Constraint Satisfaction). Under the dual update,

\limsup_{T\to\infty}\frac{1}{T}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathcal{C}(a_t,m_t)\right]\le\rho_0+O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right).

Proof. By Theorem F.2, \lambda_t\to\lambda^\star. By the saddle-point property established in Theorem G.1, the pair (\phi^\star,\lambda^\star) satisfies

\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda^\star)\le\mathcal{L}(\phi^\star,\lambda^\star)\le\mathcal{L}(\phi^\star,\lambda)\quad\forall(\phi,\lambda)\in\Phi\times\Lambda.

The saddle-point property implies both primal feasibility \mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_t\gamma^t\mathcal{C}]\le\rho_0 and complementary slackness \lambda^{\star\top}(\mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_t\gamma^t\mathcal{C}]-\rho_0)=0. Complementary slackness alone does not imply primal feasibility; it is the saddle-point property that guarantees it. The O(1/\sqrt{T}) term arises from the stochastic approximation error. \square

Remark G.1. The original formulation stated that "complementary slackness gives \lambda^{\star\top}(\mathbb{E}_\pi[\sum_t\gamma^t\mathcal{C}]-\rho_0)=0. The O(1/\sqrt{T}) term arises from the stochastic approximation error." This was a logical gap: complementary slackness alone does not imply primal feasibility of the limit. The standard first-order conditions for constrained optimisation require primal feasibility, dual feasibility, complementary slackness, and Lagrangian optimality as a set. Primal feasibility follows from the saddle-point property, not from complementary slackness alone. The corrected proof cites the saddle-point property (Theorem G.1) explicitly.

G.3 Near-Optimality

Theorem G.3 (Near-Optimality). The policy \pi_{\phi^\star} satisfies

J(\pi_{\phi^\star})\ge J(\pi^\star)-\frac{2\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1-\gamma}-O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right).

Proof. By the performance difference lemma,

J(\pi^\star)-J(\pi_{\phi^\star})=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\mathbb{E}_{(m,\rho)\sim\mu_{\pi^\star}}\left[A^{\pi_{\phi^\star}}(m,\rho,\pi^\star(m,\rho))\right].

The advantage function is bounded by the approximation error of the value function, which is O(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}/(1-\gamma)). The stochastic approximation error contributes O(1/\sqrt{T}). \square

G.4 Regret Bound

Theorem G.4 (Regret Bound). The cumulative regret satisfies

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{d\sqrt{T}}{1-\gamma}\right)+O(\sqrt{T}).

Proof. Combine Theorem E.1 with the contraction modulus \gamma. The (1-\gamma)^{-1} factor arises from infinite-horizon discounting. \square

